WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:03.180
This was a protest near the White House,
on June 1st.

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:05.580
"It has been an entirely peaceful protest..."

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:10.679
It was met by forces with helmets,
riot shields, rifles...

00:00:10.679 --> 00:00:14.259
and tear gas.

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:17.380
The authorities here were a mix of police
and military:

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:21.700
There were Secret Service, Park Police, the
National Guard, Prison Special Operations,

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:24.820
and local police from a nearby county.

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:27.920
But can you tell which ones are the police?

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:30.700
If it’s hard to tell — it’s these guys
by the way —

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:35.160
it’s because America’s police have been
looking more and more like troops.

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:37.840
"Get in the house!"

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:38.680
(screams)

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:42.020
So why do American police officers look like
soldiers?

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:47.180
And where did they get all these weapons?

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:48.860
"Don’t shoot!

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:51.180
This was a peaceful protest!"

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:59.340
In the 1980s, police in America looked more
like this.

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:02.229
The US’s crime rate had been doing this.

00:01:02.229 --> 00:01:06.290
And President Reagan called for the military
to work more directly with the police

00:01:06.290 --> 00:01:07.580
for the War on Drugs.

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:09.380
"Drugs are menacing our society."

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:12.460
"We must move to strengthen
law enforcement activities."

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:16.250
Congress agreed, and over the next few years
passed a series of bills:

00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:19.830
To give police access to military bases and
equipment,

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:23.420
for the National Guard to assist police with
drug operations,

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:26.250
for the military and police to train together,

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:29.740
and eventually, to have the military loan
police departments their excess,

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:32.280
leftover equipment, for free.

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:35.960
This would become known as the 1033 program.

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:42.380
Police departments got assault rifles like
M16s, armored trucks, and even grenade launchers.

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:46.600
And before long, it started to have an effect
on how police… police.

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:50.000
We can see that in the number of times SWAT
teams were used.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:53.220
Departments that had deployed them about once
a month in the 80s

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:57.020
were using them more than 80 times a year by 1995.

00:01:57.030 --> 00:02:01.000
Almost all of these deployments were for drug-related
search warrants,

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.950
usually forced-entry searches called “no
knock warrants.”

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:08.619
The police were becoming militarized, and
people noticed.

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:14.140
This 1997 article said it made police look
like “an occupying army.”

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:22.320
In February of 1997, two men robbed a bank
in North Hollywood, Los Angeles.

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:25.220
They had automatic rifles and body armor.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:26.960
The police didn’t.

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:34.220
By the time it ended, a dozen police officers
were injured.

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:37.940
In the aftermath of the shootout, California
police demanded they be equipped

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:41.020
with assault rifles, like the AR-15.

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:45.720
But so did police in places from Florida to
Connecticut.

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:51.020
And that same year, the 1033 program was expanded,
dropping a requirement that police departments

00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:54.310
use the equipment for drug-related enforcement.

00:02:54.310 --> 00:02:59.360
Now any law enforcement, even university police,
could access leftover military weapons,

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:01.280
for any reason.

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:05.460
A retired police chief in Connecticut told
the New York Times, “I was offered tanks,

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:08.940
bazookas, anything I wanted.”

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:12.980
Because complete records on these loans weren’t
kept until 2015,

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:16.740
we don’t know exactly how much equipment
was given out in those early years.

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:20.940
But we do have data on how much of it police
departments still have,

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:22.700
from each year it was given out.

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:27.880
And you can see a steady growth in the program
for most of the 90s and 2000s.

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:31.060
And then something happens around here.

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:35.130
“The rest of our troops in Iraq will come
home by the end of the year.”

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:39.890
In 2011, the US military formally withdrew
its troops from Iraq.

00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:44.400
That meant the military had a lot of equipment,
and one less war to use it on.

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:47.849
So it became available to the police.

00:03:47.849 --> 00:03:52.160
This is a Mine Resistant Ambush Protected
Vehicle, or MRAP.

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:56.980
It’s among the most controversial equipment
given out under the 1033 program.

00:03:56.980 --> 00:04:00.280
And we know from the data that police departments
still have several hundred of them

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:03.840
that they got in 2013 and 2014.

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:06.680
But none from 2015.

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:11.800
That’s because in August of 2014, the 1033
Program became national news.

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:15.280
"We just said 'hands up, don’t shoot,' and they
just started shooting!"

00:04:15.290 --> 00:04:19.820
A police officer in Ferguson, Missouri, had
shot and killed an unarmed black teenager

00:04:19.820 --> 00:04:21.060
named Michael Brown.

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:25.500
Afterwards, the community’s protests were
met by heavily militarized police,

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:28.360
who pointed sniper rifles at them as they marched.

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:32.240
"Tear gas and armored tanks became a familiar
sight in Ferguson, Missouri."

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:36.160
"The police departments around the country
have been getting a lot of this type of equipment..."

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:40.500
President Obama responded with an executive
order curbing the 1033 Program.

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:47.160
"We’ve seen how militarized gear can sometimes
give people a feeling like there’s an occupying force,

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:51.640
as opposed to a force that’s part of the community
that’s protecting them and serving them."

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:54.580
Two years later, President Trump’s administration
reversed that.

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:59.020
"We will not put superficial concerns
above public safety."

00:04:59.020 --> 00:05:04.080
But by that point, the 1033 Program had become
a lot less important anyway.

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:08.260
This chart shows that by 2016, most MRAPs
loaned out by the military

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:10.640
went to smaller police departments.

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:14.580
That means when larger cities today have MRAPs
and other military gear,

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:17.700
it’s often because they’ve bought it themselves.

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:21.140
And that’s because police
having military gear and weapons

00:05:21.140 --> 00:05:24.380
no longer depends on any one government program.

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:27.800
It’s now a part of how police see
themselves.

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:33.360
The thing that I think is really important
is, with that equipment comes a certain mentality.

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:34.360
This is Arthur Rizer.

00:05:34.360 --> 00:05:38.200
He’s a former military police officer,
former civilian police officer,

00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:40.580
and now studies police militarization.

00:05:40.580 --> 00:05:44.040
A big part of his research is about that mentality.

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:46.900
And he shared a poll he did of police officers with us.

00:05:46.900 --> 00:05:48.800
I asked officers,

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:53.300
do you have any problem with police officers
routinely on patrol,

00:05:53.300 --> 00:05:58.320
carrying military-grade equipment,
or dressing in military type of uniforms?

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:02.400
And the vast majority of those officers told
me, "no, I have no problem with that."

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:04.380
And then the second question I asked is,

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:09.900
do you think it changes the way that officers
feel about themselves and their role in policing?

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:13.120
And the vast majority officers, again, said
"yes."

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:17.040
And what they said was, it makes them more
aggressive, more assertive,

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:19.140
and it can make them more violent.

00:06:19.150 --> 00:06:20.920
And then finally, I asked them,

00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:24.330
how do you think the public perceives you?

00:06:24.330 --> 00:06:25.770
And the vast majority said,

00:06:25.770 --> 00:06:27.240
"it scares them."

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:29.220
They know that it scares the public.

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:32.770
They know that it makes them more aggressive
or more assertive.

00:06:32.770 --> 00:06:34.740
And that can be dangerous.

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:38.520
But they don't seem to care.

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:44.880
There are definitely times
when it's been more clearly beneficial

00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:46.979
for the police to have this equipment.

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:50.560
For example, during the Pulse
Nightclub shooting in 2016,

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:54.420
Orlando police used an armored
military vehicle to stop the shooter.

00:06:54.580 --> 00:06:57.240
But those moments tend to be the exception.

00:06:57.240 --> 00:07:04.180
Today, this equipment is still mostly used
by SWAT teams for executing drug-related search warrants.

00:07:04.180 --> 00:07:07.580
And more than half of those are still no-knock warrants,

00:07:07.580 --> 00:07:12.940
the kind that Louisville police were executing when they killed Breonna Taylor.

00:07:12.940 --> 00:07:15.080
And in the case of the Ferguson protests,

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:18.720
the Department of Justice found
that the heavily militarized presence

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:23.320
“served to escalate rather than
de-escalate the overall situation.”

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:28.320
The military, and the police, are supposed
to serve different purposes.

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:32.130
A military protects an “us” from a “them.”

00:07:32.130 --> 00:07:35.780
A police officer is supposed to be part of
the “us.”

00:07:35.780 --> 00:07:40.740
But when police think of themselves as soldiers,
that can change.

00:07:40.740 --> 00:07:47.100
What is a police officer going to do with
an assault rifle when he's facing a protest?

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:51.720
When you give someone a hammer, why are you
surprised that everything looks like a nail to them?

